{"id": "E4", "kind": "learn", "question": "Does f(x) equal exactly -3.9*x^3 - x^2/10 + 3*x (a simple closed form matching small-x deviations) or is a higher-order correction needed, tested on ordinary mid-range inputs where the two candidates diverge most?", "expect": "If f(x) = -3.9x^3 - 0.1x^2 + 3x exactly, predictions are: x=7 -> -1337.7-4.9+21=-1321.6; x=-7 -> 1337.7-4.9-21=1311.8; x=12 -> -6739.2-14.4+36=-6717.6; x=-12 -> 6739.2-14.4-36=6688.8; x=18 -> -22744.8-32.4+54=-22723.2; x=-18 -> 22744.8-32.4-54=22658.4; x=25 -> -60937.5-62.5+75=-60925; x=-25 -> 60937.5-62.5-75=60800; x=35 -> -167212.5-122.5+105=-167230; x=-35 -> 167212.5-122.5-105=166985; x=45 -> -355387.5-202.5+135=-355455; x=-45 -> 355387.5-202.5-135=355050; x=55 -> -648862.5-302.5+165=-649000; x=-55 -> 648862.5-302.5-165=648395; x=8.5 -> -2395.5-7.225+25.5=-2377.2; x=-8.5 -> 2395.5-7.225-25.5=2362.8; x=40 -> -249600-160+120=-249640; x=-40 -> 249600-160-120=249320; x=60 -> -842400-360+180=-842580; x=-60 -> 842400-360-180=841860. Deviations from these (especially growing with |x|) reveal the true correction term; agreement confirms the simple closed form and lets me lock it in.", "probe": {"x": [7.0, -7.0, 12.0, -12.0, 18.0, -18.0, 25.0, -25.0, 35.0, -35.0, 45.0, -45.0, 55.0, -55.0, 8.5, -8.5, 40.0, -40.0, 60.0, -60.0]}, "observation": {"y": [-1330.1669, 1315.1787, -6741.8123, 6686.8437, -22771.8464, 22646.7388, -61011.3488, 60764.7714, -167385.5965, 166900.2023, -355701.7187, 354894.0125, -649354.7036, 648150.2288, -2390.5153, 2365.6111, -249838.5344, 249201.0617, -842998.6353, 841562.0771]}, "model_version": 1, "model_prediction": {"y": [-1321.6784000000002, 1316.8764, -6716.7264, 6702.6144, -22722.213600000003, 22690.461600000002, -60928.25, 60867.0, -167254.5, 167134.45, -355528.35, 355329.9, -649160.6, 648864.15, -2376.6131750000004, 2369.5326750000004, -249684.80000000002, 249528.00000000003, -842802.0000000001, 842449.2000000001]}, "match": 1.0, "model_error": null}
