{"id": "E6", "kind": "learn", "question": "Is the residual error of the cubic fit a systematic even/odd component (e.g. a small x^2 or |x|^3 term) or just noise, by sampling a dense symmetric set of mid-range x values where the fitted coefficients a,b,c are most sensitive?", "expect": "If f(x) = a*x^3 + b*x^2 + c*x + d exactly, the residuals from the current model will be smooth and symmetric/antisymmetric according to the true b (even) and c (odd) terms; fitting these points will pin down a,b,c,d to high precision. If residuals look random and roughly constant in magnitude, the box is noisy and the cubic is already essentially correct, meaning no further refinement is needed and effort should shift to confirming there is no piecewise interval.", "probe": {"x": [3.5, -3.5, 4.5, -4.5, 6.5, -6.5, 9.0, -9.0, 14.0, -14.0, 20.0, -20.0, 30.0, -30.0, 42.0, -42.0, 50.0, -50.0, 70.0, -70.0]}, "observation": {"y": [-156.1165, 162.245, -346.8431, 344.5732, -1064.5247, 1051.8369, -2839.3692, 2810.5472, -10709.8005, 10636.3056, -31236.9338, 31085.1861, -105417.5043, 105063.5796, -289208.6199, 288509.5321, -487896.6265, 486903.2303, -1338538.0358, 1336582.9435]}, "model_version": 1, "model_prediction": {"y": [-158.369925, 157.16942500000002, -344.30377500000003, 342.319275, -1055.922075, 1051.781575, -2823.9012000000002, 2815.9632, -10678.0632, 10658.855200000002, -31179.6, 31140.4, -105311.1, 105222.9, -289048.75440000003, 288875.88240000006, -487711.5, 487466.5, -1338367.1000000003, 1337886.9000000001]}, "match": 1.0, "model_error": null}
