{"id": "E8", "kind": "learn", "question": "Is the residual of the cubic model a genuine even (x^2) component versus an odd (x) component, tested at small-to-mid magnitudes where the difference between the current fit and exact -3.9x^3 - x^2/10 + 3x is largest?", "expect": "If the true form is -3.9x^3 - x^2/10 + 3x, small |x| values will show a clear asymmetric even correction (e.g. x=2 gives about -31.2+6-0.4=-25.6, x=-2 about 31.2-6-0.4=24.8). The current cubic (a=-3.9018,b=-0.049,c=2.72) predicts nearly identical values, so any systematic deviation concentrated at small |x| where b*x^2 vs c*x trade off strongly will reveal whether the x^2 coefficient is really -0.1 or -0.05, and whether the linear term is 3 or 2.72. Symmetric pairs at these magnitudes isolate the even part cleanly.", "probe": {"x": [2.0, -2.0, 2.5, -2.5, 4.0, -4.0, 5.5, -5.5, 8.0, -8.0, 11.0, -11.0, 16.0, -16.0, 22.0, -22.0, 26.0, -26.0, 32.0, -32.0]}, "observation": {"y": [-25.9373, 30.9731, -54.5867, 58.8815, -243.9864, 243.0744, -642.7592, 633.1312, -1991.0145, 1970.5708, -5190.4417, 5147.119, -15992.5792, 15892.3668, -41576.4456, 41387.6214, -68627.4869, 68362.3314, -127933.8616, 127526.3497]}, "model_version": 1, "model_prediction": {"y": [-25.9704, 25.5784, -54.471875, 53.859375, -239.6192, 238.05120000000002, -635.684225, 632.7197249999999, -1979.0976, 1972.8256000000001, -5169.3048, 5157.4468, -15950.7968, 15925.7088, -41510.2424, 41462.8104, -68540.4408, 68474.1928, -127817.31840000002, 127716.9664]}, "match": 1.0, "model_error": null}
